  <h1>Where AI and automation actually help a growing business</h1>
  <p class="lead">Practical places to start with AI and workflow automation, and where to be careful.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="container article-layout">
  <article class="prose article" data-article><p>There is a lot of noise about AI. For most businesses the useful question is narrower: which repetitive tasks can we hand to software without losing quality?</p>
<h2 id="good-candidates">Good candidates</h2>
<p>Look for work that is frequent, text-heavy or rule-based:</p>
<ul><li>Answering routine customer questions from existing help content</li><li>Sorting and routing incoming emails or enquiries</li><li>Pulling key fields out of documents such as invoices or forms</li><li>Summarising long threads or reports for managers</li><li>Moving data between tools that do not talk to each other</li></ul>
<h2 id="start-with-a-single-workflow">Start with a single workflow</h2>
<p>Choose one task, measure how long it takes today, and automate it end to end. A small win builds the confidence and the knowledge needed for larger projects.</p>
<h2 id="keep-a-human-in-the-loop">Keep a human in the loop</h2>
<p>AI systems can be wrong with great confidence. For anything that affects customers or money, design a review step. Good automation makes mistakes visible and easy to correct rather than hiding them.</p>
<h2 id="test-with-your-own-examples">Test with your own examples</h2>
<p>Demonstrations on clean sample data say little about how a system will behave on your messy real documents. Ask any provider to test on a representative sample of your own material before you commit.</p>
<h2 id="think-about-data">Think about data</h2>
<p>Consider what information would be sent to third-party services and whether that is appropriate. Minimise personal and confidential data, and confirm any regulatory requirements with your own advisers.</p>
<h2 id="not-everything-needs-ai">Not everything needs AI</h2>
<p>Many &quot;intelligent&quot; improvements are really just good integration: a form that creates a CRM record, a notification when stock runs low, a report that arrives every Monday. These are cheaper, more predictable and often deliver most of the value.</p>
<p>If you have a repetitive process in mind, <a href="/contact">tell us about it</a> and we will give you an honest view on whether automation is worth pursuing.</p></article>
